'''''Pratheedhi''''', a loose garment was a part of the [[Bride|bride's]] [[Clothing|attire]] made of simple strip of [[Textile|cloth]]. Pratidhi was an [[Sewing|unstitched]] garment similar to almost all contemporary clothes that were wrapped around the body in different ways. The [[Woman|women]] were fastening it up at the back. The materials were usually animal [[Hide (skin)|skin]], [[cotton]], [[wool]], or [[silk]].<ref name=":1">{{Cite book|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=lHpDAAAAYAAJ|title=The Journal of the Bihar Research Society|date=1977|publisher=Bihar Research Society|isbn=|location=|pages=511|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Kumar|first=Raj|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=f-1XyQ2eIG8C|title=Paintings and Lifestyles of Jammu Region: From 17th to 19th Century A.D.|date=2006|publisher=Gyan Publishing House|isbn=978-81-7835-577-1|location=|pages=142|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Kumar|first=Ritu|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=qYK1AAAAIAAJ|title=Costumes and Textiles of Royal India|date=2006|publisher=Antique Collectors' Club|isbn=978-1-85149-509-2|location=|pages=331|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Ghose|first1=Vijaya|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=_v1tAAAAMAAJ|title=Tirtha, the Treasury of Indian Expressions|last2=Ramanathan|first2=Jaya|last3=Khandekar|first3=Renuka N.|date=1992|publisher=CMC Limited|isbn=978-81-900267-0-3|location=|pages=21|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Chandra|first1=Moti|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=TaHpAAAAMAAJ|title=Costumes, Textiles, Cosmetics & Coiffure in Ancient and Mediaeval India|last2=Gupta|first2=Swarajya Prakash|date=1973|publisher=Oriental Publishers|isbn=|location=|pages=8|language=en}}</ref><ref name=":0">''Shatapatha Brahmana'', y, 4.4, i</ref>

== Style ==
The Pratidhi is a garment of the [[Vedic period]] (1500 and 500 BCE). The Vedas have stated many clothes; indeed, those all were unsewn clothes and wraps in varied ways, such as [[Uttariya]], [[Adivasah]], [[Antariya|and Antariya]]. Concurrently [[Atharvaveda]] refers to ''Nivi'', ''Vavri'', ''Upavasana'', ''Kumba'', ''Usnlsa'', and Pratidhi as underwear.( RV. x. 85. 8)<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Handa|first1=O. C.|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=JFPnh9B5zncC|title=Textiles, Costumes, and Ornaments of the Western Himalaya|last2=Hāṇḍā|first2=Omacanda|date=1998|publisher=Indus Publishing|isbn=978-81-7387-076-7|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Bhandari|first=Vandana|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=nckSAQAAIAAJ|title=Costume, Textiles and Jewellery [i.e. Jewelry] of India: Traditions in Rajasthan|date=2005|publisher=Mercury Books|isbn=978-1-904668-89-3|language=en}}</ref>

These clothes were varying with the material, size, and style of wrapping and draping. Pritidhi was smaller, with one or two strips of cloth pulled over or across on the bust and tied on the back.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite book|last1=Nair|first1=Rukmini Bhaya|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=u6XFDwAAQBAJ|title=Keywords for India: A Conceptual Lexicon for the 21st Century|last2=deSouza|first2=Peter Ronald|date=2020-02-20|publisher=Bloomsbury Publishing|isbn=978-1-350-03925-4|location=|pages=|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite book|last=Kumar|first=Raj|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=f-1XyQ2eIG8C&dq=pratidhi&pg=PA142|title=Paintings and Lifestyles of Jammu Region: From 17th to 19th Century A.D.|date=2006|publisher=Gyan Publishing House|isbn=978-81-7835-577-1|location=|pages=142|language=en}}</ref>

